mh2lm: scale PM8150B MSOC and inspect PMIC IRQ lines (0123)

`sweetlog-0112m16.bin` shows that the existing `soc <= 100` test rejects
PM8150B's real raw monotonic SOC bytes (normally 0xa8..0xac), then publishes a
voltage-curve estimate every two seconds. Cable insertion changes the pack
terminal voltage by tens of millivolts, which appears in the UI as a false
multi-point capacity jump.

Linux's `fg_get_msoc_raw()` reads BATT_SOC_FG_MONOTONIC_SOC (0x4009) and its
shadow copy (0x400a), rejecting a mismatched pair. `fg_get_msoc()` then maps raw
0..255 to 0..100, retaining 0/255 endpoints and scaling 1..254 to 1..99 with
nearest-integer rounding. Mirror that read-only behaviour. The generic voltage
curve remains only a read-failure fallback, and the normal log prints both
values for hardware validation.

Extend the existing `sweetbattirqdiag=1` preflight with read-only configuration
samples for USBIN_PLUGIN (USB peripheral bit 4) and CHG_STATE_CHANGE (CHGR
peripheral bit 1): real-time, edge, polarity, enable and latch state. This does
not register GIC SPI 481, clear a latch, change PMIC interrupt enables or write
any charging register. It separates PMIC line configuration from the
already-recorded PMIC-arb APID routing before a later event-controller change.

